[ ] Specify or forbid a plan_path shared by several workspaces $each workspace sees the other's collections as unowned-plans-collection, quarantines them, and doctor fix may offer to delete them; every workspace also owns next and inbox collections, so names collide too. Found 2026-09-15. DEFERRED 2026-09-18, not decided: this needs investigation before a call is safe — does any real config actually share a plan_path across two clearhead workspaces today (additional_workspaces entries are separate repos with their own plans/ dirs; when would they collide)? Only once that's confirmed is it safe to decide forbid (validate at load, error out) vs support (design real cross-workspace collection ownership/namespacing) and write it into specifications/workspace.md. Out of scope for tonight's platform+specifications overnight pass; needs a clearhead-core investigation session first.$ ^2026-09-15T01:24 #01a0a42b-3be7-7691-bcbf-366e1fd87a77
[ ] Give the run report levels distinct jobs and lead with the blocker $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md. The runbook requires the report to live in the work rather than in chat, which is right, but it produced the same facts four times: a long action description, a charter log line, a per-task checkpoint note, and a final checkpoint -- all prose summaries of one thing. Cost: writing and reading the same analysis three extra times, with drift risk between the copies. Three changes, all to the runbook and its task template. (1) Distinct roles, not copies: the action description is the full authoritative analysis; the charter log line is one line naming the decision and the commit, never a recap; the per-task checkpoint is machine-shaped (task id, commit, status); the final checkpoint is an index of those, not a narrative. (2) A stopped task's description must LEAD with the decision it needs -- a NEEDS DECISION line naming the question -- then the analysis. Two tasks stopped that night with the analysis appended after the original context, so the first thing a reader meets is the old description, not the blocker. (3) Findings outside the run's scope must still be filed as actions in the owning charter. The rule against unilaterally resolving docs-versus-log source-of-truth is right, but the run had no channel for an out-of-bounds finding, so a real one (four dead wiki-links in this charter) would have ended up only in agent-local scratchpad memory, where the project cannot see it. Acceptance: the next run's artifacts hold one full analysis, one-line logs, machine-shaped checkpoints, stopped tasks whose first line is the question, and zero findings that exist only in chat or agent memory. Note: this edits the same file as the separately-owned premise-field change, so land them together.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T10:16 #01a0b585-68d2-721c-a2da-c07fc0353c30
[ ] Attribute lens findings to the turn, not the whole file $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md. Four dead wiki-links in .clearhead/charters/support.md (lines 10, 69 and 70; present since commit 88799f81 on 2026-08-23) were reported as must-fix on roughly seven consecutive turns. The run did not introduce them: the only edit to that file was an appended log line, which put the whole file into the turn's delta, which surfaced every finding in it. That is structural rather than unlucky, because ClearHead's capture loop always appends to a charter document, so every log entry in any repository can trigger it. Three fixes in the linter, cheapest first. (1) Attribute by hunk: diff the file against HEAD for the turn and report findings only on changed lines; that alone removes the whole class. (2) If that is too coarse, add a suppression ledger that survives turns and carries provenance -- finding id, path, line, reason, who acknowledged it, when -- so the next turn reports 'four acknowledged, zero new' rather than a fresh stop. (3) Scope rules the repository deliberately does not follow to non-test code: the rust-unwrap advisory fired repeatedly on test modules where unwrap is the established convention, which trains the reader to ignore advisories. Note: the target is the agent harness linter rather than a ClearHead repository, so this is about making the tool trustworthy; it is filed here because it surfaced through dogfooding and because the capture loop is what triggers it.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T10:16 #01a0b585-68da-7037-a9d5-42f1ee1628ce
[ ] Split charter document identity from domain identity $DECIDED 2026-09-18 with the user. The file-level charter document has an OPTIONAL id, since a person may not have written it yet. The domain Charter REQUIRES one. Strictness increases at the conversion into the domain type. That conversion takes the id as an argument supplied by the shell (as Workspace from_parts already does for ephemeral_id), so clearhead-core stays pure: no clock or RNG inside parse_charter or assembly, which reverses the Uuid now_v7 added in dd20473. Resolution order: frontmatter id, then sidecar charter id, then the shell-supplied ephemeral id, never persisted by a read. Only normalize stamps a missing id, as the deliberate you-may-add-stuff pass, matching actions. Creation of a whole new document (jot on a missing file, close on an implicit charter) also writes the id, as add does for actions. No other write verb stamps as a side effect. Write verbs need no id at all: they edit document text and never round-trip through the domain Charter. Known trade-off: an id-less charter has a different identity each load, which only affects identity consumers such as query IRIs, and the charter-document-without-id lint plus doctor keep it visible. Three ordered children below. Origin: review of the overnight run, findings 1 to 3.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T11:03 #01a0b5b0-17a8-73c8-878a-cdc69737e59d
    >[ ] Stamp missing charter ids in normalize and point the doctor lint at it $Third child. normalize adds a frontmatter id to any charter document that declares none, as the deliberate identity-minting pass, mirroring actions. Change the charter-document-without-id finding text, which currently names clearhead update charter as the remedy, to name normalize. Test that normalize is idempotent and that doctor is clean afterwards. DOCS: decided 2026-09-18 that the spec stays implementation-agnostic and carries no paragraph on this, so the behavior is documented with the commands. In the same commit as the behavior, document in clearhead-cli README.md and docs/UI.md that normalize is the pass that stamps charter ids, and that update, close and jot never stamp one on an existing document (jot and close stamp only when they create the document). Neither file mentions normalize today.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:03 <01a0b5b0-17b8-77e8-82e9-971c194e64f9 #01a0b5b0-17d2-717c-938d-74ae3d848ce1
[ ] Tidy the overnight run review nits $Four small findings from the review of the overnight run. One: write_collection_displaynames uses a bare atomic_write instead of the EffectBatch seam. Two: in update_charter the displayname write runs after the md write, so a failure there returns an error for a write that already landed. Three: validate_source_path for a not-yet-existing target trusts the deepest existing ancestor, so a path such as charters/nope/../../../x.actions passes lexically and should be rejected by normalizing the path first. Four: write_charter_document has a dangling empty doc comment line.$ !4 ^2026-09-18T11:03 #01a0b5b0-17eb-7573-9921-dd40c4212d25
[ ] Adopt a single-source rule for where each kind of knowledge lives $Found 2026-09-18: one decision was written into an action, a charter log, DECISIONS.md, a runbook and a memory file, which is how drift starts, and Decision 38 already cites a gate that does not exist. Write the rule into docs/CONTRIBUTING.md: decision text lives only in DECISIONS.md; actions hold task state and link to the decision; charter logs hold dated findings, not decisions; memory holds only what is not derivable from the repo; agent-workspace holds cited beliefs. Add the promotion path the agent-workspace versus clearhead boundary decision asks for: a finding worth keeping is promoted from the workspace to a clearhead jot or action on purpose, and nothing else copies it. Optionally add a doctor or okf lint that flags a decision heading in an action description.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:39 #01a0b5d0-da38-74c4-bd29-ab0ecda10946
[ ] Give agent-facing verbs typed input and output\: show as JSON, text from stdin $Found 2026-09-18. show action and show charter print tables only, so an agent cannot read an action back as data; and add or update take long notes as command-line arguments, so a note with backticks, dollar signs or brackets is fought through shell quoting and the grammar. Add a JSON output form to show, and let add, update and jot read the note from stdin or a file flag. This is the CLI half of the typed-input problem and benefits any harness with a shell. Related: the append-description and newline actions; do them as one pass over the note path.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:39 #01a0b5d0-da47-714b-9a4e-113403989cea
[ ] Add an independent review step to unattended runs $Found 2026-09-18. The overnight run passed every gate and still shipped a real compare-and-swap gap (update and close charter captured their revision after computing the new text); tests covered the mechanism, not the invariant. Add to the runbook template a review step before each submodule bump: a fresh agent, ideally a different model, reviews the diff against the invariants in the plan and reports findings, and a finding blocks the bump. Also add invariant-level tests where a mechanism test would not catch the break: no read verb writes, no write verb stamps an id, a change between read and write is a conflict for every charter verb.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:39 #01a0b5d0-da56-771e-afe7-12a0d08720b7
[ ] Add an agent-nvim launcher\: one shared read-only headless instance per worktree $Decision 41. Script (suggest scripts/agent-nvim with start, stop and status) that runs nvim headless with the users config on a socket keyed by worktree path, opens a Rust file so rust-analyzer attaches, and reports the connection target for the nvim MCP. One instance per worktree, shared by all agents there, never the humans editor. Read-only by policy: queries only, no lsp_apply_edit or rename. Measured 2026-09-18: rust-analyzer about 2GB plus nvim about 180MB, so stop must be easy and status should show memory. The LSP client name is rust_analyzer with an underscore; passing a hyphenated name makes wait_for_lsp_ready hang until it times out. Also finish the experiment: time warm queries, which was not measured.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T12:29 <01a0b5ff-3cd7-72b5-9e5c-9857bc0b19b3 #01a0b5ff-3ce5-737e-b63f-7d736455a8da
[ ] Add the navigation and batching rules to the runbook template $Decision 41 and the analysis of the overnight export. Add to the runbook template and the overnight runbook: navigate code with the shared read-only language server and its compact views, grep only for text; batch independent tool calls in one turn (287 of 320 overnight turns made a single call); checkpoint per task, not once at the end. The plan doc crate-merge-and-charter-identity already carries the first two lines in its working protocol.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T12:29 <01a0b5ff-3ce5-737e-b63f-7d736455a8da #01a0b5ff-3cf2-76e1-83b1-7f0aae63fc70
[ ] Update the Structurizr model for the crate merge $Found 2026-09-21 during a docs cleanup pass, same drift class as the resource.rs workspace-lock doc fix. workspace.dsl and source-entries.dsl still model clearhead-workspace-fs and clearhead-lsp as separate crates/containers: workspace.dsl:22 groups native adapters under 'clearhead-workspace-fs', workspace.dsl:57 describes clearhead-lsp as not depending 'on the CLI' (now misleading -- it is a second binary target of the same clearhead-cli crate), workspace.dsl:60 names a 'Workspace filesystem adapter' component technology as clearhead-workspace-fs, and source-entries.dsl has 5 dead GitHub blob URLs under crates/clearhead-workspace-fs/src/* and crates/clearhead-lsp/src/* that 404 post-merge. Already fixed in this pass (prose only, no model/DSL changes): README.md Index of Repositories, structurizr/documentation/01-current-architecture.md, docs/workflows.md (2 sequence diagrams), clearhead-core/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. Left the DSL model itself alone deliberately: restructuring containers/components/relationships needs a deliberate pass against the actual current crate boundary (clearhead-cli now owns filesystem+CLI+LSP as one crate, two binaries) plus regenerating workspace.json and checking source-entries.dsl links resolve, not a blind rename inside a docs cleanup pass. Touch points: structurizr/workspace.dsl, structurizr/source-entries.dsl, structurizr/workspace.json (regenerate/export step, check structurizr/README.md and compose.yaml for how).$ !4 ^2026-09-21T20:29 #01a0c729-5de2-77cc-ad47-9ffc021e3ad3
